  "response": "The Tasaday case was adjudicated less by a single decisive test than by the gradual reopening of the field to independent observers and by the discipline's willingness to argue the question in public, with material and linguistic evidence weighed against the testimony of those who had managed access to the group. Both the claim of pristine isolation and the charge of outright hoax had to be measured against what could actually be documented on the ground.\n\nThe Tasaday were presented to the world in 1971 as a previously uncontacted forest-dwelling group in South Cotabato, Mindanao. Manuel Elizalde Jr. headed PANAMIN, the body that controlled outside access to the Tasaday. The first reported contacts with them had been made rather earlier, in the late 1960s, by a T'boli hunter and trapper named Dafal, well before the group was unveiled to journalists and scientists. The Tasaday numbered about 250 people when they were first brought to public attention. Because PANAMIN held that gatekeeping power, the Tasaday area was closed to outside researchers in 1976. That decade of enforced inaccessibility, which left the original claims untested, in turn produced a 1986 ABC television documentary titled \"The Tribe That Never Was,\" which alleged that the Tasaday were a fabrication. The broadcast came after the Swiss journalist Oswald Iten had reached the Tasaday caves in 1986, in the months following the fall of the Marcos government.\n\nThe Tasaday were wearing manufactured shirts and trousers when Iten arrived at the caves in 1986. Thomas Headland's organization of a symposium on the Tasaday controversy at the 1989 International Congress of Anthropological and Ethnological Sciences in Zagreb led directly to the volume he edited, \"The Tasaday Controversy: Assessing the Evidence,\" which the American Anthropological Association published in 1992. The linguist Lawrence Reid conducted fieldwork on the speech variety used by the Tasaday. Two histories were on offer and exactly one of them could hold: either the Tasaday had never encountered people from outside their forest before 1971, or they had contact with neighbouring lowland and upland groups before that date, there being no third possibility and no way for both to obtain. The manufactured clothing worn at the caves indicates the second of these, namely that contact with lowland and upland neighbours had indeed occurred well before 1971. Reid's comparative material, however, establishes that the Tasaday speech variety is unrelated to any Manobo language, and such a linguistic isolate cannot be squared with a history of regular dealings with those neighbouring populations.\n\nJudgments in a dispute of this kind rest on the ordinary instruments of anthropology: material traces observable at the site itself, the comparative classification of speech, and the public weighing of rival testimony before a professional audience. They do not rest on the authority of whoever first presents a people to the world, nor on the vividness of the image that presentation creates.",
